  Engineering observation
    Throughput does not scale: at 64 threads it reaches 0.205× the single-thread rate, and scaling
    efficiency falls to 0.003. Measured CPU utilisation never exceeds 1.65 of 10 available cores,
    i.e. barely more than one core is ever busy.

    The artifact attributes this to the runtime, not the design: concurrency_model = "python
    threads (GIL-bound reference decision path)". Under CPython the Global Interpreter Lock
    serialises the pure-Python decision path, so adding threads adds contention and
    context-switching without adding parallel execution.

    Separating the two limitations

    Implementation limitation  — what IS measured here
      The CPython GIL serialises this reference implementation's pure-Python decision path. Real,
      reproducible, and disclosed above.

    Architecture limitation  — what is NOT measured here
      Whether the L-DREA decision path is inherently unparallelisable. This experiment cannot
      answer that: separating the two would require running the same decision path on a GIL-free
      runtime. No claim is made in either direction.

  Honesty: This negative result is reported in full rather than omitted. Safety — the property the
           paper claims — holds at every thread level. Throughput scaling is not claimed.


