║ EXPERIMENT 5/8   Component Ablation                                                              ║
║ Is every architectural component necessary?                                                      ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

  Purpose
    Remove each structural control one at a time and measure how many denials it converts into
    permits ('leaked permits'). This is the causal evidence that every component contributes to
    safety and none is redundant.

  Scientific motivation
    Layered safety architectures invite the objection that the layers are decorative. An ablation
    converts that from an aesthetic argument into a measurement: remove a control, count the
    unsafe actions that now get through.

  Why this experiment exists
    Supplies the counterfactual that a single end-to-end pass cannot: how many permits would have
    leaked WITHOUT each component.

  Reviewer concern addressed
    Reviewer R6  (Comment #5)
      "The architecture may be over-engineered; component necessity is unproven."

  Paper sections
    IX (ablation)  ·  Table I (ablation rows)

  Benchmark
    Ablation harness (frozen engine, controlled deficit workload)
  Inputs
    • Workload : 60,000 deterministic decisions per configuration
    • Configurations : baseline, -class-veto, -non-compensatory-Gamma, -authorization-layer
  Metrics produced
    → Permits / denials per configuration
    → Leaked permits vs baseline
    → Leak rate + Wilson 95% CI
    → Risk difference
    → Cohen's h
    → Throughput per configuration
    → Full latency distribution per configuration
    → Replay consistency per configuration
  Tables produced
    ▦ table1_primary_metrics.md
  Figures produced
    ◪ fig_component_ablation.svg

  Artifact provenance
  Calculated now
    ✓ Leaked permits per removed component
    ✓ Leak rate + Wilson CI
    ✓ Throughput per config
    ✓ Replay consistency per config
  Reused (not recomputed)
    ↺ the frozen evaluate_decision as the baseline
  Generated
    → ablation.json
    → ablation.csv
    → ablation_log.jsonl

  Execution steps
    ▸ Building deficit workload
    ▸ Running each ablated config
    ▸ Counting leaked permits

  Expected scientific interpretation
    A non-zero leak count is direct causal evidence that the removed component was load-bearing on
    this workload. A zero leak count does NOT prove a component is useless — the replay layer
    leaks 0 permits because it is an audit control, not a decision gate; its contribution is
    provenance (E2), not leakage prevention. The contrasts are deterministic, so the risk
    difference is exact and no significance test applies.

