║ EXPERIMENT 7/8   AgentDojo Runtime Governance                                                    ║
║ Does the guard stay sound on an external, author-independent adversarial corpus?                 ║
╚══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝

  Purpose
    Test the guard on a third-party adversarial benchmark. Every real prompt-injection attack
    target is submitted straight to the frozen boundary (no LLM needed) to measure whether any
    genuinely-foreign attacker target is ever permitted, and recorded episodes are re-analyzed for
    permit/deny/stability/overhead.

  Scientific motivation
    Results on a corpus the authors mapped themselves are weak evidence of generalisation.
    AgentDojo is an external benchmark with attacks the authors did not design, which makes a zero
    false-permit result on its attacker targets a genuinely independent soundness measurement.

  Why this experiment exists
    Answers the strongest reviewer objection: that L-DREA is tuned to ULB.

  Reviewer concern addressed
    Reviewer R8  (Comment #7)
      "Results may not generalize beyond the authors' own dataset."

